Let R be a ring with a multiplicative identity 1. Let U be a right ideal of R.
We are given that R has exactly two right ideals. These ideals are {0} and R itself.

I. R is commutative.
Consider the ring Z of integers. The only right ideals of Z are {0} and Z. Z is commutative.
Consider the ring M_2(R) of 2x2 matrices with entries in a ring R. If R is not commutative, then M_2(R) has more than two right ideals. So, if R has only two right ideals, it is not necessarily commutative.
However, if R is a division ring, then R is a local ring, and the only right ideals are {0} and R.

II. R is a division ring.
If R is a division ring, then every non-zero element has a multiplicative inverse. This implies that the only right ideals are {0} and R.
If R is a division ring, then for any non-zero element r, the set {r} is a right ideal. Since R has only two right ideals, it must be that every non-zero element is in R. Thus, R is a division ring.

III. R is infinite.
Consider the ring Z/2Z. The right ideals are {0} and Z/2Z. Z/2Z is finite. So, R is not necessarily infinite.

Since R has exactly two right ideals, {0} and R, it must be a division ring.
If R is a division ring, then every non-zero element has a multiplicative inverse.
If R is a division ring, then R is a local ring.
If R is a local ring, then the only right ideals are {0} and R.
If R is a division ring, it is not necessarily commutative. For example, the ring of quaternions is a division ring but not commutative.
If R is a division ring, it is not necessarily infinite. For example, the finite field F_p where p is a prime is a division ring.

Therefore, only II must be true.

